The nucleus is lithium with 3 neutrons: that is \( ^6\text{Li} \) (3 protons, 3 neutrons) or \( ^7\text{Li} \) (3 protons, 4 neutrons)? The problem says "Li with 3 neutrons", so that means 3 protons + 3 neutrons = mass number 6. So it's \( ^6\text{Li} \).  

The kinetic energy per nucleon for a particle moving at speed \( v = 0.96c \) is:  
\[
\gamma = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - 0.96^2}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - 0.9216}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{0.0784}} = \frac{1}{0.28} \approx 3.57142857
\]  
Total energy of the nucleus: \( E = \gamma m c^2 \).  
Mass of \( ^6\text{Li} \) is about 6 u, where 1 u = 931.494 MeV/c². So rest energy ≈ 6 × 931.494 MeV = 5588.964 MeV ≈ 5.588964 GeV.  
Total energy = γ × rest energy ≈ 3.57142857 × 5.588964 GeV ≈ 19.9606 GeV.  
Kinetic energy = total - rest ≈ 19.9606 - 5.588964 = 14.3716 GeV.  

